This package builds the llama.cpp backend of the CUDA runtime, without model
weights or the npm web UI. Use an OpenAI-compatible client with
llama-kvmem-server.
The patched llama/ggml libraries are statically linked into the two tools, so
this package can coexist with sci-misc/llama-cpp and prism-llama-cpp.

Upstream also ships NInfer and PolyStrata backends; they are not built here.

For RTX 5080, select the architecture in /etc/portage/env/kvmem-llama-cpp:
    CMAKE_CUDA_ARCHITECTURES="120a-real"
and add to /etc/portage/package.env:
    sci-misc/kvmem-llama-cpp kvmem-llama-cpp

Download Qwen3.8-27B-GSQ-RCO-IQ3_S-mtp.gguf separately from:
https://huggingface.co/ISTA-DASLab/Qwen3.8-27B-GSQ-RCO-GGUF

The upstream 16 GiB IQ3 recipe uses a bounded GPU KV working set with a
256K logical context. Historical KV is stored in system RAM, and optionally on
SSD through the shared RAM/SSD tiers that replaced the retired NVMe store.
Start with:
    llama-kvmem-server -m /path/to/Qwen3.8-27B-GSQ-RCO-IQ3_S-mtp.gguf \
        --host 127.0.0.1 --port 18200 -c 262144 --gpu-layers all \
        --kvmem --kvmem-budget 36864 --kvmem-gen-reserve 1024 \
        --kvmem-host-mib 16384 \
        -ctk q8_0 -ctv q8_0 --spec-type draft-mtp --no-ui

--kvmem-gen-reserve is reusable generation workspace, not a cap on how much
output a request may produce; context and request limits still apply. The
host-memory quota is shared by every conversation, so it has to hold all the
concurrent working sets. Add --kvmem-disk-mib N and --kvmem-disk-path DIR to
put inactive history on SSD; the two are only accepted together.

Check the upstream README for current sampling and memory settings. Model
inference correctness must be checked after changing the CUDA compiler; a
successful build alone does not validate IQ3 output.
